            <div class="physics-wall-copy">
                <span class="physics-wall-alert">CRITICAL LIMIT REACHED</span>

                <h3 class="physics-wall-topic">
                    800mm² Reticle Limit &amp; Defect Density
                </h3>

                <p class="physics-wall-description">
                    Moore's Law demanded more transistors, but the lithography
                    machines hit a hard physical ceiling: the 800mm² reticle
                    limit. Lenses could only project light onto an area so
                    large. As monolithic dies grew to fill this entire printable
                    space, they encountered a devastating statistical reality:
                    defect density.
                </p>

                <p class="physics-wall-description">
                    In a cleanroom, a single microscopic speck of dust is
                    catastrophic. When your entire chip relies on one massive
                    piece of silicon, one defect destroys the entire unit,
                    destroying yield rates and rendering the manufacturing
                    process unprofitable. On advanced nodes, public estimates
                    place a 300mm wafer at about $17,000; once yield loss and
                    packaging value are considered, a failed near-reticle-class
                    chip can mean $10,000+ in wasted product value.
                </p>

                <p class="physics-wall-transition">
                    This is the wall chiplets were built to climb: instead of
                    risking one giant die, the next section shows how AMD split
                    the design into smaller, higher-yielding pieces.
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- RIGHT: MONOLITHIC FAILURE VISUAL -->
            <div class="physics-wall-monitor">
                <div class="physics-wall-monitor-header">
